ads are about 165bp. They widened 30bp in Q3 2026, from roughly 135bp.\n- The market is open. Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\".\n- Norvane's 2027 capex is planned up 12%, and it looks able to fund that through debt at wider spreads.\n- Pellham's 2027 capex is planned up 18%, and it probably can't fund that through debt alone. It will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes \"until financing improves\". The sources don't say why, so ratings or leverage limits as the cause is my inference.\n- Depth beyond roughly C$1B per deal is unverified.\n\n**Equity**\n- The issuance window is poor. Pellham withdrew a C$2.4B offering because demand fell short. Two other utility share offerings were cut back in Q3 2026.\n- Equity can supply a partial, opportunistic share at best. A small, steady at-the-market programme is the likelier route if the window reopens. That is my inference.\n- A reopened window, or a smaller or staged raise from Pellham, would change this.\n\n**Gaps**\n- I found no total C$ capex need or funding gap.\n- I found no ratings, FFO/debt, net debt/EBITDA or covenant data.\n- I found no 2027 maturity schedule.\n- I found no valuation data or at-the-market capacity figures.\n- I found no spreads by issuer, and nothing on whether the widening continued into Q4.\n\n**Implication for grid equipment:** Pellham is the largest grid-equipment buyer in Caldria, so its cuts are a downside risk to demand.\n\nSources: n5.r1 (debt, from n9–n12) and n6.r1 (equity).","ttft_ms":1693,"type":"result","duration_ms":53926,"uuid":"5c0e747d-370e-4651-a95f-cf6fbe753ccc","ttft_stream_ms":1258,"time_to_request_ms":362,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":37,"system_prompt":23,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":17,"input_attachments":54,"input_hooks":8,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":16,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":16,"init_frame_send":14,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":18,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"aut